    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d395">

                <pb facs="0696" n="662" sameAs="frus:frus1948v02/pg662"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Control (Germany)/12–1348:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the United States Political Adviser for Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Murphy</hi>),
                        <hi rendition="#italic">at Berlin</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-12-17T19:00:00-05:00">December 17,
                            1948—7 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1983. Re Benelux request for copy draft Occ Statute (urtel.2931, Dec 13; Deptel 281,
                    Dec 15 to Frankfort; London’s tel 5269 Dec 16 to Dept rptd Berlin as 668<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d395-f1"/>), Dept received yesterday formal requests from
                    Benelux missions citing para 4 Annex A of final report London talks on Ger, June
                        1948,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d395-f2"/> and continuing that Belg and Lux Govts
                    attach utmost importance to full opportunity for final consultation on
                    matter.</p>
                <p>Dept received yesterday <hi rendition="#italic">Aide-Mémoire</hi> from Swiss
                        Leg<anchor n="3" xml:id="d395-f3"/> questioning whether Art 2<hi
                        rendition="#italic">f</hi> and Art 5 para 16 might be of discriminatory
                    nature toward Swiss interests. Contents <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Aide-Mémoire</hi> sent you yesterday by airgram.</p>
                <p>Since Swiss apparently have obtained copy of draft Occ Statute, or at any rate
                    have exact knowledge of certain provisions, Dept inclined now to believe that
                    copy of draft Occ Statute in present form should <pb facs="0697" n="663"
                        xml:id="pg663"/> be given Benelux. If Clay concurs, request arrangements be made with Fr and Brit
                    authorities for delivery copies to Benelux.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d395-f4"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lovett</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d395-f1" n="1" xml:id="d395-f1-content">
                <p>None printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d395-f2" n="2" xml:id="d395-f2-content">
                <p>Annex A of the Report on the London Conference on Germany, June 1, (<ref
                        target="#pg309">p. 309</ref>) is the same as document TRI/10, March 6, <ref
                        target="#pg144">p. 144</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d395-f3" n="3" xml:id="d395-f3-content">
                <p>The Swiss <hi rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi>, dated December 15, is not
                    printed (740.00119 Control (Germany)/12–1548).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d395-f4" n="4" xml:id="d395-f4-content">
                <p>In telegram 2950, December 18, from Berlin, not printed, Ambassador Murphy explained that General Clay’s original objection to furnishing the
                    Benelux countries with copies of the draft statute was the fear that the Benelux
                    would wish to participate in the finalization of the agreement and would become
                    involved in the remaining disagreements. Inasmuch as the matter of the draft
                    statute had been passed on to the governmental level, however, General Clay felt a copy of the draft could be
                    furnished to the Benelux provided it came from the governments concerned and not
                    from the military governors in Germany (740.00119 Control (Germany)/12–1848).
                    Acting on instructions from the Department, Ambassador Douglas in London subsequently furnished the
                    Benelux representatives with a copy of the draft statute.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
